1. What is a Kubernetes downgrade?
A downgrade means moving from a newer Kubernetes version to an older version.

Imagine you upgraded your control plane from v1.31.2 to v1.32.0, but after the upgrade, your team discovers an application compatibility issue.

Your team decides to roll back to v1.31.2.

- Important, bro: Kubernetes does not support a general in-place downgrade using kubeadm upgrade in the same way it supports upgrades. You need to follow a version-specific recovery or rollback plan. Simply installing an older kubeadm package is not enough.

2. How do we handle a downgrade in real life?
There are two different situations.

Case A: You upgraded the packages, but haven't completed the cluster upgrade.

You may be able to restore the previous package versions or rerun the supported recovery procedure. The correct steps depend on what has already changed.

Case B: You successfully upgraded Kubernetes and now want the previous version back.

This is more complicated. You generally need a tested recovery plan, potentially including rebuilding the control plane or restoring a compatible etcd snapshot. Restoring an old snapshot also rolls back the Kubernetes objects stored in that snapshot, so changes made afterward can be lost.